[39.320273, "o", "\u001b[29A\u001b[1G\u001b[K\u001b[90m  Provider: anthropic-vertex\u001b[39m\r\r\n\u001b[1G\u001b[K\u001b[90m  Model: claude-haiku-4-5\u001b[39m\r\r\n\u001b[1G\u001b[K\u001b[90m  Working directory: /tmp/ami-demo-bHk7bO\u001b[39m\r\r\n\u001b[1G\u001b[K\u001b[90m  Session: session-20261113-125647-387-0\u001b[39m\r\r\n\u001b[1G\u001b[K\u001b[90m  Permissions: auto-allow\u001b[39m\r\r\n\u001b[E\u001b[1G\u001b[K\u001b[90m  Tip: Read the docs at \u001b]8;;https://superinference.ai/docs\u0007https://superinference.ai/docs\u001b]8;;\u0007\u001b[39m\r\r\n\u001b[1G\u001b[K\r\r\n\u001b[1G\u001b[K\u001b[2m\u2298 Indexing skipped: no project root found\u001b[22m\r\r\n\u001b[1G\u001b[K\r\r\n\u001b[1G\u001b[K\u001b[48;5;235m\u001b[38;5;255m > Write a Python script called maze_solver.py that generates a small random 15\u00d715 maze using \u001b[39m\u001b[49m\r\r\n\u001b[1G\u001b[K\u001b[48;5;235m\u001b[38;5;255mrecursive backtracking (seed it with 42 for reproducibility), then solves it from top-left to \u001b[39m\u001b[49m\r\r\n\u001b[1G\u001b[K\u001b[48;5;235m\u001b[38;5;255mbottom-right using BFS, and prints the maze with the solution path marked. Then write a concise \u001b[39m\u001b[49m\r\r\n\u001b[1G\u001b[K\u001b[48;5;235m\u001b[38;5;255mREADME.md documenting what the script does, the algorithms used, and how to run it. Finally run the \u001b[39m\u001b[49m\r\r\n\u001b[1G\u001b[K\u001b[48;5;235m\u001b[38;5;255mscript and cat the README. \u001b[39m\u001b[49m\r\r\n\u001b[1G\u001b[KI'll create a maze solver script with recursive backtracking generation and BFS solving, then write\r\r\n\u001b[1G\u001b[Kthe documentation.\r\r\n\u001b[1G\u001b[K\r\r\n\u001b[1G\u001b[K\u001b[2m[Checkpoint cp-1794571046185-1: /tmp/ami-demo-bHk7bO/maze_solver.py]\u001b[22m\r\r\n\u001b[1G\u001b[K\r\r\n\u001b[1G\u001b[K  \u001b[38;5;167m\u25cf\u001b[39m \u001b[1m\u001b[38;5;167mfile_edit\u001b[39m\u001b[22m\u001b[2m \u00b7 \u001b[22m\u001b[38;5;243m/tmp/ami-demo-bHk7bO/maze_solver.py\u0